  <h1>Common Spousal Support Misconceptions</h1>
  <p><em>Reviewed by Harper Quinn (HQ), Editor-in-Chief &amp; Family Law Lead. Updated May 2026.</em></p>

  <p>Both paying and receiving spouses frequently misunderstand spousal support rules in ways that affect their negotiating positions and litigation strategy. The most common and consequential mistaken beliefs are addressed below.</p>

  <h2>&ldquo;Only husbands pay alimony.&rdquo;</h2>
  <p><strong>Wrong.</strong> Modern state statutes are entirely gender-neutral. The higher-earning spouse pays and the lower-earning spouse receives, regardless of gender. The percentage of female payers has grown significantly over the past two decades as women increasingly out-earn their spouses. Courts evaluate income, earning capacity, and contributions to the marriage — not gender — in making support determinations.</p>

  <h2>&ldquo;Alimony is always permanent.&rdquo;</h2>
  <p><strong>Rarely.</strong> Permanent alimony is increasingly uncommon. Most states now favor time-limited rehabilitative or durational support designed to help the lower-earning spouse transition to economic self-sufficiency, not to maintain a lifetime income stream. Permanent (or open-duration) support typically requires long marriages &mdash; often 20+ years &mdash; combined with a significant income gap and limited earning capacity for the recipient. Florida abolished permanent alimony outright in 2023. Most other states treat open-ended support as a last resort rather than a default.</p>

  <h2>&ldquo;Alimony stops automatically at retirement.&rdquo;</h2>
  <p><strong>Usually no.</strong> Retirement is a basis to seek modification, but it does not automatically terminate an ongoing support obligation. The paying spouse must file a modification petition with the court, show that retirement was taken in good faith at an appropriate age (not strategically early to reduce support), and demonstrate that the changed financial circumstances warrant a reduction or termination. Some states (most notably New Jersey under its 2014 reform) have created statutory presumptions that open-duration alimony terminates when the payor reaches full Social Security retirement age, but even there, a court order is required to formally terminate or modify. Continue paying support until the court modifies the obligation; stopping without a court order creates arrears.</p>

  <h2>&ldquo;I can deduct my alimony payments on my federal taxes.&rdquo;</h2>
  <p><strong>Wrong for post-2018 divorces.</strong> The Tax Cuts and Jobs Act of 2017 eliminated the federal alimony deduction for divorce or separation agreements executed after December 31, 2018. Payers now pay support with fully after-tax dollars. Recipients receive support as non-taxable income. For divorces finalized before that date, the old rules (deductible to payer, taxable to recipient) still apply and will continue to apply to those agreements unless they are modified to adopt the new treatment. State tax treatment varies independently; check your state&rsquo;s rules separately.</p>

  <h2>&ldquo;My ex&rsquo;s affair will mean they pay more alimony.&rdquo;</h2>
  <p><strong>Usually no.</strong> The vast majority of US states have eliminated marital fault as a factor in spousal support calculations. California, Florida, New York, Illinois, Massachusetts, Texas &mdash; none of these major states considers fault in the support analysis. A handful of Southern states (including South Carolina, Georgia in limited circumstances, and others) may consider fault in some situations, but even in fault states, its effect on the financial outcome is usually modest. Focus on the income, earning capacity, and marriage length factors rather than conduct.</p>

  <h2>&ldquo;My prenup will automatically save me from paying alimony.&rdquo;</h2>
  <p><strong>Maybe, but not automatically.</strong> Properly executed prenuptial agreements limiting or waiving spousal support are generally enforceable in most states &mdash; but enforceability is not guaranteed. Common challenges: the agreement was signed under duress or without adequate time for review; there was insufficient financial disclosure about each party&rsquo;s assets and income; one party did not have independent legal counsel (not required in most states, but strengthens the agreement&rsquo;s position); or enforcement would be unconscionable given dramatically changed circumstances. Complete waivers of support may be unenforceable in some states if they would leave the recipient eligible for public assistance. Have any prenuptial agreement reviewed by an attorney &mdash; both before signing it and before relying on it in divorce proceedings.</p>

  <h2>&ldquo;If we just lived together unmarried, I can&rsquo;t be sued for support.&rdquo;</h2>
  <p><strong>Generally right in most states, but not everywhere.</strong> Cohabitation without marriage typically does not create spousal support obligations in most US jurisdictions. However, California&rsquo;s &ldquo;Marvin doctrine&rdquo; (from <em>Marvin v. Marvin</em>, 1976) recognizes &ldquo;palimony&rdquo; claims for cohabitating couples based on express or implied agreements to share property or provide support. A few other states have recognized similar claims in specific circumstances. If there was an explicit or implicit agreement &mdash; particularly around one party giving up employment or career advancement in reliance on promises of financial support &mdash; the risk increases. Consult an attorney in your state if this situation applies.</p>

  <h2>&ldquo;My ex&rsquo;s remarriage automatically ends support.&rdquo;</h2>
  <p><strong>Often right &mdash; for the recipient&rsquo;s remarriage.</strong> Most state statutes provide that the payer&rsquo;s support obligation terminates automatically when the recipient remarries, though the payer should still obtain a formal court order confirming termination to avoid future disputes. Cohabitation with a new partner (short of marriage) may reduce or terminate support in some states, but the threshold varies significantly. Some states require proof that the cohabitation creates a supportive economic relationship reducing the recipient&rsquo;s financial need; others treat cohabitation more narrowly. The payer must typically petition the court to address cohabitation&rsquo;s effect on support rather than stopping payments unilaterally.</p>

  <h2>&ldquo;The formula is binding on the judge.&rdquo;</h2>
  <p><strong>Usually no.</strong> Formula guidelines in most states are starting points, not binding rules. Judges are authorized to deviate with documented reasoning in either direction &mdash; awarding more or less than the formula based on the specific statutory factors applicable in their jurisdiction. This judicial discretion is especially significant for permanent support amount and duration. The implication for both sides: the formula output is a reference point for settlement discussions, not a guaranteed outcome at trial. A judge who finds the formula result unjust given the specific facts will order something different, and appellate courts give family court judges substantial deference in support determinations.</p>

  <h2>&ldquo;Stay-at-home parents have weak spousal support cases.&rdquo;</h2>
  <p><strong>Wrong &mdash; often the opposite.</strong> Courts in most states give substantial weight to non-monetary contributions to the marriage, including homemaking, primary childcare, and career sacrifices made to support the other spouse&rsquo;s professional advancement. A spouse who left a career (or never pursued one) to raise children for 15 years while the other built a high-earning career has a compelling support claim in most jurisdictions. The non-monetary contribution factor and the earning capacity gap created by years out of the workforce together create a strong factual case for significant and durable support. The duration of the marriage and the extent of the career sacrifice are the key variables.</p>

  <h2>&ldquo;I can pay support in goods or services instead of money.&rdquo;</h2>
  <p><strong>Wrong.</strong> Spousal support must be paid in money. Courts award ongoing support as a monetary obligation, and arrears are calculated in dollars. In property division, parties can sometimes structure arrangements where property transfers offset future support obligations (a lump-sum equivalent), or where the payor provides in-kind housing by maintaining mortgage payments on the family home. But an ongoing support award ordered by a court is a monetary obligation — you cannot satisfy it by providing services, maintaining property, or paying expenses directly. Any non-cash arrangement must be formalized in the settlement agreement and incorporated into the court order to have legal effect.</p>

